<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Integration by parts (Section 5.4)</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Find antiderivatives of these functions</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \int x\sin x~dx</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          Choose <m>u=x</m> and <m>dv=\sin x~dx</m> so that
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u = x \amp du=dx \\
            dv = \sin x~dx \amp v = -\cos x \\
            \end{array}
          </md>.
          Then
          <md>
            \int x\sin x~dx = -x\cos x + \int \cos x~dx =
            -x\cos x + \sin x
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\ds \int x^2\ln x~dx</m>.  Hint:  try <m>u=\ln x</m> and
          <m>dv=</m> whatever is left over.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          This is a little strange, but we know how to differentiate
          <m>\ln x</m> and we don't know how to antidifferentiate it
          so: 
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u=\ln x \amp du = \frac1x ~dx \\
            dv = x^2 ~dx \amp v=\frac13 x^3 \\
            \end{array}
          </md>.
          Then
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int x^2\ln x~dx \amp =
              \frac13 x^3\ln x - \frac13 \int x^3\frac1x ~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = 
              \frac13 x^3\ln x - \frac13 \int x^2 ~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = 
              \frac13 x^3\ln x - \frac19 x^3
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \int x\sqrt{1-x^2}~dx</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          Looks like a substitution may work better here:
          <m>u=1-x^2</m> so that <m>du=-2x~dx</m>.  Then
          <md>
            \int x\sqrt{1-x^2}~dx = -\frac12\int\sqrt{u}~du =
            -\frac 23 u^{3/2} = -\frac 23(1-x^2)^{3/2}+C
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \int x^2e^{-2x}~dx</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          Let's try integration by parts so that
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u = x^2 \amp du = 2x~dx \\
            dv = e^{-2x}~dx \amp v = -\frac12 e^{-2x} \\
            \end{array}
          </md>.
          Then
          <md>
            \int x^2e^{-2x}~dx = -\frac12 x^2e^{-2x} + \frac12\int
            xe^{-2x}~dx
          </md>.
          Now we need to use integration by part again!
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int x^2e^{-2x}~dx \amp = -\frac12 x^2e^{-2x} + \frac12\int
              xe^{-2x}~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -\frac12 x^2e^{-2x} -\frac14xe^{-2x} + \frac14 \int
              e^{-2x}~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -\frac12 x^2e^{-2x} -\frac14xe^{-2x} - \frac18
              e^{-2x}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
            
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \int \arctan x~dx</m>.  (Suggestion:  let
        <m>u=\arctan x</m> and <m>dv = 1~dx</m>.)</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          Following the suggestion, we have
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u = \arctan x \amp du =\frac1{1+x^2}~dx \\
            dv = 1~dx \amp v = x \\
            \end{array}
          </md>.
          This means that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int \arctan x~dx \amp = x\arctan x - \int\frac
              x{1+x^2}~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = x\arctan x - \frac 12\ln|1+x^2|
            </mrow>
          </md>
          where the last antiderivative is found using a substitution
          <m>w=1+x^2</m> as in our last class meeting.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.75in">
    <statement>
      <p>Evaluate the definite integral <m>\displaystyle \int_1^3 t\ln
      t~dt</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="07">
      <p>
        As in the earlier example we worked out,
        <md>
          \begin{array}{ll}
          u = \ln t \amp du = \frac 1t~dt \\
          dv = t~dt \amp v = \frac12 t^2 \\
          \end{array}
        </md>
        so an antiderivative is
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int t\ln t~dt \amp = \frac12 t^2\ln t - \frac12 \int t~dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac12 t^2\ln t - \frac14 t^2
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        This means that
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_1^3 t\ln t~dt \amp = \left(\frac12 t^2\ln t - \frac14
            t^2\right)\Biggl|_1^3
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac12 \cdot 9\ln 3 - \frac 14\cdot 9 - \frac12\ln
            1 - \frac14
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \frac92 \ln 3 - 2
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Here are a few more antiderivatives for practice.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \int \ln t~dt</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          As we have seen,
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u = \ln t \amp du = \frac1t~dt \\
            dv = dt \amp v = t \\
            \end{array}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            \int \ln t~dt = t\ln t - \int \frac 1t\cdot t~dt = t\ln t
            - \int 1dt = t\ln t - t
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \int \frac {x}{1+x^2}~dx</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          We have a variety of
          techniques now so we need to think about which one applies.
          This looks like a substitution if we let <m>u=1+x^2</m>.
          Then <m>du=2x~dx</m> so that
          <md>
            \int \frac {x}{1+x^2}~dx = \frac12 \int \frac 1u~du =
            \frac12 \ln |u| = \frac12 \ln|1+x^2|
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that <m>f(t) = te^{-3t}</m> and that <m>F(x) =
      \int_0^x f(t)~dt</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>F'(x)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          The Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus says that
          <md>
            F'(x) = f(x) = xe^{-3x}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find an expression for <m>F(x)</m> that doesn't involve
        an integral.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          We will use integration by parts with
          <md>
            \begin{array}{ll}
            u = t \amp du = ~dt \\
            dv = e^{-3t}~dt \amp v = -\frac13 e^{-3t}
            \end{array}
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \int te^{-3t}~dt \amp
              = -\frac13 te^{-3t} + \frac13 \int e^{-3t} =
              -\frac13 te^{-3t} - \frac19 e^{-3t}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              F(x) \amp = \left(-\frac13 te^{-3t} -
              \frac19 e^{-3t}\right)\Biggl|_0^x =
              -\frac13 xe^{-3x} - \frac19e^{-3t} + \frac19
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Is <m>F</m> an increasing or decreasing function when
        <m>x&gt;0</m>?  Explain your 
        thinking.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="07">
        <p>
          <m>F</m> is always increasing on that interval because
          <m>F'(x) = f(x)=xe^{-3x}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
